NAME
       ssh-copy-id - install your public key on another computer for key logins

SYNOPSIS
       ssh-copy-id [-f] [-i [IDENTITY-FILE]] [-p PORT] [-o SSH-OPTION]
                   [USER@]HOST

DESCRIPTION
       ssh-copy-id logs in to HOST with ssh (normally with the account's
       password, one last time) and appends your public key to
       ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote account. It creates ~/.ssh and the
       file when they are missing and gives them safe permissions (700 for
       the directory, 600 for the file), which sshd insists on.

       After that, ssh USER@HOST logs in with the key and no longer asks for
       the account's password.

       ssh-copy-id first tries to log in with the key. Keys that already work
       are not added a second time, so running it twice is harmless.

       Without -i, it copies the newest of your default public keys
       (~/.ssh/id_*.pub). You need a key pair first: create one with
       ssh-keygen.

OPTIONS
       -i FILE
              Copy this public key (FILE or FILE.pub) instead of the default.

       -f     Force: copy the key without first checking whether it already
              works.

       -p PORT
              Connect to this port instead of 22.

       -o OPTION=VALUE
              Pass an option to ssh, such as -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no.

EXAMPLES
       Set up key logins from server1 to student on server2:

           ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
           ssh-copy-id student@server2
           ssh student@server2 hostname

       Copy a particular key to root on server2:

           ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/lab_key.pub root@server2

NOTES
       The key is installed for the remote account named in the command, not
       for your local one: ssh-copy-id student@server2 lets the local user log
       in to server2 as student.

       If key logins still ask for a password, check the permissions of the
       remote home directory, ~/.ssh and authorized_keys, and their SELinux
       context (restorecon -Rv ~/.ssh).

SEE ALSO
       ssh-keygen(1), ssh(1), sshd_config(5)
